//! stage:typing no-std
// expected (premsubst): rejected, as today. `U is P` is found by the requirement on `U`; the caller
// only knows `T.X is Q` through the requirement on `T`, a different conformance, whose `X` may be a
// different type even though `T == U` (compare Tests/CompilerTests/negative/associated-type-inequality.hylo).
// review: the first version rejects this only because the witness of `U is P` obtained by coercing
// T's requirement gives a projection that never matches (DESIGN.md 3.7). Once coercions are removed,
// it stays rejected only under the commit rule (3.10); without that rule it is accepted through T's
// conformance.
// observed: (eq25, premsubst as first designed) FULL+premsubst rejected (bound=1 released=2); +selfcheck same; +assocsubst rejected; NEW rejected
// currently: base rejected (subgoals=9 matches=664): no coercion from '(P<U>, Q<(U::P).X>) ==> [Void]() let -> Void' to '[Void]() let -> Void'; FULL rejected (subgoals=5 matches=368): no coercion from '(P<U>, Q<(U::P).X>) ==> [Void]() let -> Void' to '[Void]() let -> Void'
trait P { type X }
trait Q {}
fun f<V where V is P, V.X is Q>() {}
fun g<T, U where T == U, T is P, U is P, T.X is Q>() { f<U>() }
